summary:
The authors take the flare current sheet from an ultra-high-resolution 2.5D ARMS breakout eruption (the Guidoni et al. 2016 run). They use its upstream conditions to run kglobal, which yields electron and proton spectra with κ≈2.9 (Fig. 2), most acceleration occurring within about 24 s. They inject these into a RADYN+FP loop built from an ARMS reconnected field line. Injection follows a leaky-box exponential decay with τi=20 s, chosen explicitly to match typical QPP periods (Hayes et al. 2020). FP is extended to transport multiple species coupled through the return current. Results include:
- a ~24–28 MK looptop flash from return-current Ohmic heating in the first ~1 s;
- evaporation upflows falling from 1.7 Mm/s to 160 km/s;
- condensation of 10–30 km/s;
- protons undergoing warm-target collisions in the corona and stopping sharply at the transition region;
- pitch-angle-dependent electron fates under return-current, collisional and mirroring forces (Figs. 10–11);
- co-spatial 30–50 keV HXR emissivity and white-light enhancement about 690 km above the photosphere, matching Krucker et al. (2015) limb observations.
For STIX/EUI timing work, it is a physics-context and forward-modelling resource rather than an observational or time-series analysis. It models only a single injection episode in one symmetric loop.
